Q: Iterator in a doubly linked list in C A: <p>In my opinion, this function <code>list_iter_t *list_createiter(list_t *list);</code> doesn't need to return a pointer to dynamically allocated data.</p>
<p>Simply declaring it as <code>list_iter_t list_createiter(list_t *list);</code> would be much more efficient.</p>
<p>The reasons are as follows:</p>
<ol>
<li>It avoids memory fragmentation;</li>
<li>No need to keep track of create/destroy calls. As its a stack-allocated value, you don't need to destroy it;</li>
<li>All other functions receive the reference of the iterator already (as a pointer), so no need to change everything else; You just need to call functions using &amp; operator, for example:</li>
</ol>
<pre><code>list_t *list;

// [...] create list

list_iter_t iter = list_createiter(list); 
int succ = list_hasnext(&amp;iter);

// [...] destroy list. No need to destroy iterator.
</code></pre>

